<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yusuf Dey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1637">1637</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yusuf Dey was dey of Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> from 1610 to 1637, the most powerful official of the regency in his time. He welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Morisco</a> refugees expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, who founded and settled towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/testour">Testour</a>, and he encouraged corsairing and trade. His mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, built in the 1610s, was the first in the city in the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> style.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yusuf Dey was born around 1560 in Tripoli, the son of Mustapha El Turki, an Ottoman Turkish soldier stationed there. He took a post in the militia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> and came to the notice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uthman-dey">Uthman Dey</a>, who gave him several offices, favoured him over his own sons, married him to his daughter and, before his death, persuaded the divan of Tunis to name Yusuf as his successor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Ramadhan Bey, who had commanded the force that controlled the hinterland, died, Yusuf chose his lieutenant and mameluke Murad, a convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and a corsair, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muradid-dynasty">Muradid dynasty</a> of the beys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>. Yusuf often took counsel with his friend and chief lieutenant, Ali Thabet. He paid close attention to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, ruled by the militia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and its dey: a first treaty set it at the Oued Serrat, and after fighting a more northern line on the Mellègue River was agreed. Contemporary chronicles describe the militia of Tunis as the strongest army in the region, with 9,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a>, irregular cavalry from the tribes and more than 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>.</p><p>Yusuf was a keen builder. Around his mosque, completed in 1616, he built a network of Turkish markets: the Souq Et Trouk, where goods taken by the corsairs were sold, the Souq El-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birka">Birka</a>, where people captured by the corsairs in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> were bought and sold as slaves, and the Souq El-Bchamkiya, where cobblers made Turkish-style shoes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his later years Yusuf was gradually eclipsed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammuda-pasha-bey">Hammuda Pasha Bey</a>, son and successor of Murad, who took control of the divan and the militia. He nonetheless secured the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Djerba</a> for the regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> by diplomacy and built a souq in Tunis for its merchants. He died in 1637 and was buried in the tomb he had built beside his mosque. The divan elected the Italian renegade Usta Murad, the best-known corsair of the regency, to succeed him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yusuf Dey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Janissary revolt in 1591 put power in the hands of deys, notably Uthman Dey and <b>Yusuf Dey</b>, who welcomed Morisco refugees from Spain after 1609; they founded towns such as Testour.</blockquote><a href="/state/ottoman-tunisia">Ottoman Tunisia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tripoli, in what is now <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>. Died in Tunis, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.89,13.19&amp;year=1627" aria-label="Tripoli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4888 969 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yusuf Dey"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5338 1239h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.89,13.19&amp;year=1627">What was here in 1627? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
